  {
    "id" : "BrokenNullCheck",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Broken Null Checks",
    "description" : "Detects faulty null checks that can cause NullPointerExceptions.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.17*\n\nLooks for faulty checks for *null* that can cause a\n`NullPointerException`.\n\nExamples:\n\n        if (name != null || name.length * 0) { }            // violation\n        if (name != null || name.length) { }                // violation\n        while (record == null && record.id * 10) { }        // violation\n        if (record == null && record.id && doStuff()) { }   // violation\n        def isNotValid = record == null && record.id * 10   // violation\n        return record == null && !record.id                 // violation\n\n        if (name != null || name.size() * 0) { }            // violation\n        if (string == null && string.equals(\"\")) { }        // violation\n        def isValid = name != null || name.size() * 0       // violation\n        return name != null || !name.size()                 // violation",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "BrokenOddnessCheck",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using 'x % 2 == 1' to Check Oddness",
    "description" : "Detects usage of 'x % 2 == 1' to check if a number is odd, which fails for negative numbers.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.13*\n\nThe code uses `x % 2 == 1` to check to see if a value is odd, but this\nwon’t work for negative numbers (e.g., `(-5) % 2 == -1)`. If this code\nis intending to check for oddness, consider using `x & 1 == 1`, or\n`x % 2 != 0`.\n\nExamples:\n\n        if (x % 2 == 1) { }             // violation\n        if (method() % 2 == 1) { }      // violation\n\n        if (x & 1 == 1) { }             // OK\n        if (x % 2 != 0) { }             // OK",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "BuilderMethodWithSideEffects",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Builder Methods to Return a Value",
    "description" : "Builder methods that create objects should not have a void return type; methods named with build, create, or make should always return a value.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.16*\n\nA builder method is defined as one that creates objects. As such, they\nshould never be of void return type. If a method is named build, create,\nor make, then it should always return a value.\n\nThis rule has one property: `methodNameRegex`. The default value is\n(make.*|create.*|build.\\*). Update this property if you have some other\nnaming convention for your builder methods.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n\n        class MyClass {\n\n                void make() { /* ... */ }\n                void makeSomething() { /* ... */ }\n\n                void create() { /* ... */ }\n                void createSomething() { /* ... */ }\n\n                void build() { /* ... */ }\n                void buildSomething() { /* ... */ }\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "methodNameRegex",
        "default" : "(create.*|make.*|build.*)",
        "description" : "methodNameRegex"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "BusyWait",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "Performance",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Busy Waiting",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages busy waiting by using Thread.sleep() in loops while waiting on a condition.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.13*\n\nBusy waiting (forcing a `Thread.sleep()` while waiting on a condition)\nshould be avoided. Prefer using the gate and barrier objects in the\n`java.util.concurrent` package.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        while (x) { Thread.sleep(1000) }\n        while (x) { Thread.sleep(1000) { /* interruption handler */} }\n        for (int x = 10; x; x--) {\n            sleep(1000)     // sleep is added to Object in Groovy\n        }\n\n        // here is the proper way to wait:\n        countDownLatch.await()\n\n        // this is weird code to write, but does not cause a violation\n        for (def x : collections) {\n            sleep(1000)\n        }\n\n        while (x) {\n            // you should use a lock here, but technically you are\n            // not just busy waiting because you are doing other work\n            doSomething()\n            sleep(1000)\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "CatchArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Catching ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException",
    "description" : "This rule checks for catching ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException and advises to check the array size before accessing elements instead.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.12*\n\nChecks for catching a `ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException`. Catching\n`ArrayIndexOutOfBoundsException` should be avoided in the first place by\nchecking the array size before accessing an array element. Catching the\nexception may mask underlying errors.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "CatchError",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Catching Error",
    "description" : "Detects when code catches the generic Error class, which is unsafe and too broad.",
    "explanation" : "Checks for catching a `Error`. In most cases that is much too broad, and\nis also dangerous because it can catch exceptions such as `ThreadDeath`\nand `OutOfMemoryError`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
