    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "EnumCustomSerializationIgnored",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Defining Custom Serialization Methods or Fields in Enums",
    "description" : "Detects enums that define writeObject(), writeReplace() methods or declare serialPersistentFields or serialVersionUID fields, which are ignored during enum serialization.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.19*\n\nChecks for enums that define `writeObject()` or `writeReplace()`\nmethods, or declare `serialPersistentFields` or `serialVersionUID`\nfields, all of which are ignored for enums.\n\nFrom the javadoc for `ObjectOutputStream`:\n\n*The process by which enum constants are serialized cannot be\ncustomized; any class-specific writeObject and writeReplace methods\ndefined by enum types are ignored during serialization. Similarly, any\nserialPersistentFields or serialVersionUID field declarations are also\nignored–all enum types have a fixed serialVersionUID of 0L.*\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        enum MyEnum {\n            ONE, TWO, THREE\n            private static final long serialVersionUID = 1234567L               // violation\n            private static final ObjectStreamField[] serialPersistentFields =   // violation\n                { new ObjectStreamField(\"name\", String.class) }\n            String name;\n\n            Object writeReplace() throws ObjectStreamException { .. }      // violation\n            private void writeObject(ObjectOutputStream stream) { .. }     // violation\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "EqualsAndHashCode",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Overriding Both equals() and hashCode() Methods",
    "description" : "Ensures that if a class overrides either the equals(Object) or hashCode() method, it must override both methods.",
    "explanation" : "Checks that if either the `boolean equals(Object)` or the\n`int hashCode()` methods are overridden within a class, then both must\nbe overridden.\n\nHere is an example of code that produces a violation:\n\n        class MyClass {\n            boolean equals(Object object) {\n                // do something\n            }\n        }\n\nAnd so does this:\n\n        class MyClass {\n            int hashCode() {\n                return 0\n            }\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "EqualsOverloaded",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Overloading equals Method",
    "description" : "Detects when a class defines an equals method whose parameter is not of type Object, resulting in overloading rather than overriding equals.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.14*\n\nThe class has an `equals` method, but the parameter of the method is not\nof type `Object`. It is not overriding `equals` but instead overloading\nit.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        class Object1 {\n            //parameter should be Object not String\n            boolean equals(String other) { true }\n        }\n\n        class Object2 {\n            // Overloading equals() with 2 parameters is just mean\n            boolean equals(Object other, String other2) { true }\n        }\n\n        class Object3 {\n            // a no-arg equals()? What is this supposed to do?\n            boolean equals() { true }\n        }\n\n\n        // all of these are OK and do not cause violations\n        class Object4 {\n            boolean equals(Object other) { true }\n        }\n\n        @SuppressWarnings('EqualsOverloaded')\n        class Object5 {\n            boolean equals(String other) { true }\n        }\n\n        class Object6 {\n            boolean equals(java.lang.Object other) { true }\n        }\n\n        class Object7 {\n            boolean equals(other) { true }\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExceptionExtendsError",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Extending Error",
    "description" : "This rule detects when a class extends from Error, which represents system exceptions and should not be extended by user code.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.13*\n\nErrors are system exceptions. Do not extend them.\n\nExamples:\n\n        class MyError extends Error { }  // violation\n        class MyError extends java.lang.Error { }  // violation\n\n        class MyException extends Exception { }  // OK",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExceptionExtendsThrowable",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Extending Throwable Directly",
    "description" : "This rule checks for classes that extend Throwable directly. Custom exception classes should extend Exception or its subclasses instead.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.21*\n\nChecks for classes that extend `Throwable`. Custom exception classes\nshould subclass `Exception` or one of its descendants.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        class MyException extends Throwable { }   // violation",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExceptionNotThrown",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce Throwing Exception After Construction in Catch Block",
    "description" : "Checks that an exception constructed within a catch block is actually thrown as the last statement.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.18*\n\nChecks for an exception constructor call without a `throw` as the last\nstatement within a catch block. This rule treats any constructor call\nfor a class named *xxx***Exception** as an exception constructor call.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        void execute() {\n            try { } catch(Exception e) { new Exception(e) }     // violation\n        }\n\n        try {\n            doStuff()\n        } catch(DaoException e) {\n            log.warning(\"Ooops\", e)\n            new ServiceException(e)                             // violation\n        } catch(Exception e) {\n            new SystemException(e)                              // violation\n        }\n\n        try {\n            doStuff()\n        } catch(Exception e) { throw new DaoException(e) }      // ok",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitArrayListInstantiation",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit ArrayList Instantiation",
    "description" : "This rule checks for the explicit instantiation of an ArrayList using the no-argument constructor in Groovy code.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule checks for explicit calls to the no-argument constructor of\n`ArrayList`. In Groovy, it is best to write `new ArrayList() as []`,\nwhich creates the same object.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitCallToAndMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Call to and() Method",
    "description" : "Detects when the and(Object) method is called directly instead of using the & operator in Groovy code.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects when the `and(Object)` method is called directly in\ncode instead of using the `&` operator. A groovier way to express this:\n`a.and(b)` is this: `a & b`. This rule can be configured to ignore\n`this.and(Object)` using the *ignoreThisReference* property. It defaults\nto *true*, so even `and(x)` will not trigger a violation. The default is\n*true* because `and` appears commonly in Grails criteria.\n\nThis rule also ignores all calls to `super.and(Object)`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreThisReference",
        "default" : "true",
        "description" : "ignoreThisReference"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitCallToCompareToMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Call to compareTo() Method",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages direct calls to the compareTo(Object) method, recommending the use of Groovy comparison operators (<=>, >, >=, <, <=) instead.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects when the `compareTo(Object)` method is called directly\nin code instead of using the \\*=\\*, \\*, \\*=, \\*, and \\*= operators. A\ngroovier way to express this: `a.compareTo(b)` is this: `a \\*\\=\\* b`, or\nusing the other operators. Here are some other ways to write groovier\ncode:\n\n        a.compareTo(b) == 0               // can be replaced by: a == b\n        a.compareTo(b)                    // can be replaced by: a *=* b\n        a.compareTo(b) * 0                // can be replaced by: a * b\n        a.compareTo(b) *= 0               // can be replaced by: a *= b\n        a.compareTo(b) * 0                // can be replaced by: a * b\n        a.compareTo(b) *= 0               // can be replaced by: a *= b\n\nThis rule can be configured to ignore `this.compareTo(Object)` using the\n*ignoreThisReference* property. It defaults to `false`, so even\n`compareTo(x)` will trigger a violation.\n\nThis rule also ignores all calls to `super.compareTo(Object)`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreThisReference",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "ignoreThisReference"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitCallToDivMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Call to div() Method",
    "description" : "Detects and discourages direct calls to the div(Object) method instead of using the / operator in Groovy code.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects when the `div(Object)` method is called directly in\ncode instead of using the `/` operator. A groovier way to express this:\n`a.div(b)` is this: `a / b`. This rule can be configured to ignore\n`div.xor(Object)` using the *ignoreThisReference* property. It defaults\nto *false*, so even `div(x)` will trigger a violation.\n\nThis rule also ignores all calls to `super.div(Object)`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 2,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreThisReference",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "ignoreThisReference"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitCallToEqualsMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Call to equals() Method",
    "description" : "Detects direct calls to the equals(Object) method in code instead of using the Groovy idiomatic operators '==' or '!='.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects when the `equals(Object)` method is called directly in\ncode instead of using the `==` or `!=` operator. A groovier way to\nexpress this: `a.equals(b)` is this: `a == b` and a groovier way to\nexpress : `!a.equals(b)` is: `a != b`. This rule can be configured to\nignore `this.equals(Object)` using the *ignoreThisReference* property.\nIt defaults to *false*, so even `equals(x)` will trigger a violation.\n\nThis rule also ignores all calls to `super.equals(Object)`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreThisReference",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "ignoreThisReference"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "ExplicitCallToGetAtMethod",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "CodeStyle",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Explicit Call to getAt Method",
    "description" : "Detects direct calls to the getAt(Object) method instead of using the Groovy [] index operator.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects when the `getAt(Object)` method is called directly in\ncode instead of using the `[]` index operator. A groovier way to express\nthis: `a.getAt(b)` is this: `a[b]`. This rule can be configured to\nignore `this.getAt(Object)` using the *ignoreThisReference* property. It\ndefaults to *false*, so even `getAt(x)` will trigger a violation.\n\nThis rule also ignores all calls to `super.getAt(Object)`.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "ignoreThisReference",
        "default" : "false",
        "description" : "ignoreThisReference"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
