  {
    "id" : "JavaIoPackageAccess",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "Compatibility",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using java.io Package for File System Access in Enterprise JavaBeans",
    "description" : "This rule detects usage of the java.io package to access files or the file system within Enterprise JavaBeans, which violates the EJB specification.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.14*\n\nThis rule reports violations of the Enterprise JavaBeans specification\nby using the java.io package to access files or the file system.\n\nThe Enterprise JavaBeans specification requires that every bean provider\nfollow a set of programming guidelines designed to ensure that the bean\nwill be portable and behave consistently in any EJB container \\[1\\].\n\nIn this case, the program violates the following EJB guideline: “An\nenterprise bean must not use the java.io package to attempt to access\nfiles and directories in the file system.”\n\nA requirement that the specification justifies in the following way:\n“The file system APIs are not well-suited for business components to\naccess data. Business components should use a resource manager API, such\nas JDBC, to store data.”\n\nREFERENCES - \\[1\\] Standards Mapping - Common Weakness Enumeration -\n(CWE) CWE ID 576 - \\[2\\] The Enterprise JavaBeans 2.1 Specification Sun\nMicrosystems\n\nBy default, this rule is not applied to tests and test cases.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        FileSystem.getFileSystem()          // any method on FileSystem\n        FileSystem.fileSystem.delete(aFile) // property access of FileSystem\n\n        // shouldn't create files\n        new File(name)\n        new File(name, parent)\n\n        // don't create file readers\n        new FileReader(name)\n\n        // don't create file output streams\n        new FileOutputStream(name)\n        new FileOutputStream(name, true)\n\n        // don't create random access file\n        new RandomAccessFile(name, parent)",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "doNotApplyToFilesMatching",
        "default" : ".*(Spec|Test|Tests|TestCase)\\.groovy",
        "description" : "doNotApplyToFilesMatching"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JdbcConnectionReference",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Direct Use of java.sql.Connection",
    "description" : "Detects direct use of java.sql.Connection in application code, which is discouraged.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.15*\n\nChecks for direct use of `java.sql.Connection`, which is discouraged and\nalmost never necessary in application code.\n\nFor a more *Groovy* alternative, see\n<http://groovy-lang.org/databases.html> for information on the **Groovy\nSql** abstraction layer for JDBC/SQL.\n\nNote: If a violation is triggered from an **import** statement, then you\nmay get multiple violations per import if there are multiple classes in\nthe source file. In that case, the imports are processed once per class.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JdbcResultSetReference",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Direct Use of java.sql.ResultSet",
    "description" : "Detects direct usage of java.sql.ResultSet which is unnecessary when using Groovy's Sql facility or an ORM framework like Hibernate.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.15*\n\nChecks for direct use of `java.sql.ResultSet`, which is not necessary if\nusing the Groovy **Sql** facility or an ORM framework such as\n*Hibernate*.\n\nSee <http://groovy-lang.org/databases.html> for information on the\n**Groovy Sql** abstraction layer for JDBC/SQL.\n\nNote: If a violation is triggered from an **import** statement, then you\nmay get multiple violations per import if there are multiple classes in\nthe source file. In that case, the imports are processed once per class.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JdbcStatementReference",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Direct Use of Jdbc Statement Classes",
    "description" : "Detects direct usage of java.sql.Statement, java.sql.PreparedStatement, or java.sql.CallableStatement instead of using Groovy Sql or ORM frameworks.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.15*\n\nChecks for direct use of `java.sql.Statement`,\n`java.sql.PreparedStatement`, or `java.sql.CallableStatement`, which is\nnot necessary if using the Groovy **Sql** facility or an ORM framework\nsuch as *Hibernate*.\n\nSee <http://groovy-lang.org/databases.html> for information on the\n**Groovy Sql** abstraction layer for JDBC/SQL.\n\nNote: If a violation is triggered from an **import** statement, then you\nmay get multiple violations per import if there are multiple classes in\nthe source file. In that case, the imports are processed once per class.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 10,
    "parameters" : [
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitAssertAlwaysFails",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid JUnit Assertions That Always Fail",
    "description" : "Detects JUnit assert() calls with constant arguments that cause the assertion to always fail, such as assertTrue(false) or assertFalse(true).",
    "explanation" : "Rule that checks for JUnit `assert()` method calls with constant or\nliteral arguments such that the assertion always fails. This includes:\n\\* `assertTrue(false)` \\* `assertTrue(0)` \\* `assertTrue('')` \\*\n`assertTrue([])` \\* `assertTrue([:])` \\* `assertFalse(true)` \\*\n`assertFalse('abc')` \\* `assertFalse(99)` \\* `assertFalse([123])` \\*\n`assertFalse([a:123)` \\* `assertNull(CONSTANT)`. \\* `assertNull([])`. \\*\n`assertNull([123])`. \\* `assertNull([:])`. \\* `assertNull([a:123])`.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitAssertAlwaysSucceeds",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid JUnit Assertions That Always Succeed",
    "description" : "Detects JUnit assert() method calls with constant arguments that cause the assertion to always succeed, such as assertTrue(true), assertFalse(false), and assertNull(null).",
    "explanation" : "Rule that checks for JUnit `assert()` method calls with constant\narguments such that the assertion always succeeds. This includes: \\*\n`assertTrue(true)` \\* `assertTrue(99)` \\* `assertTrue('abc')` \\*\n`assertTrue([123])` \\* `assertTrue([a:123])` \\* `assertFalse(false)` \\*\n`assertFalse('')` \\* `assertFalse(0)` \\* `assertFalse([])` \\*\n`assertFalse([:)` \\* `assertNull(null)`\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitAssertEqualsConstantActualValue",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Using Constant as Actual Value in JUnit AssertEquals",
    "description" : "Detects usages of JUnit's assertEquals where the actual parameter is a constant or literal, which is likely a mistake where the expected and actual parameters are swapped.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.19*\n\nReports usages of\n`org.junit.Assert.assertEquals([message,] expected, actual)` where the\n`actual` parameter is a constant or a literal. Most likely it was\nintended to be the `expected` value.\n\nNOTE: This is a [CodeNarc Enhanced Classpath\nRule](./codenarc-enhanced-classpath-rules.html). It requires\n**CodeNarc** to have the application classes being analyzed, as well as\nany referenced classes, on the classpath.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        assertEquals(result, 2)\n        assertEquals(\"Message\", result, 2)\n        assertEquals(result, 2.3d, 0.5d)\n        assertEquals(\"Message\", result, 2.3d, 0.5d)",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToFilesMatching",
        "default" : ".*(Spec|Test|Tests|TestCase)\\.groovy",
        "description" : "applyToFilesMatching"
      },
      {
        "name" : "compilerPhase",
        "default" : "4",
        "description" : "compilerPhase"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitFailWithoutMessage",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Enforce JUnit fail() Calls Include Message",
    "description" : "This rule detects calls to JUnit's fail() method without an argument and enforces providing a failure message for better error reporting.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects JUnit calling the `fail()` method without an argument.\nFor better error reporting you should always provide a message.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitLostTest",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Detect JUnit 4 Test Methods Missing @Test Annotation",
    "description" : "Detects public instance void no-argument methods named 'test*' in classes importing JUnit 4 that lack the @Test annotation.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.18*\n\nThis rule checks for classes that import JUnit 4 classes and contain a\n`public`, instance, `void`, no-arg method named \\*test\\*\\* that is not\nabstract and not annotated with the JUnit 4 `@Test` annotation.\n\nNote: This rule should be disabled for Grails 2.x projects, since the\nGrails test framework can use AST Transformations to automatically\nannotate test methods.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        import org.junit.Test\n\n        class MyTestCase {\n            void testMe() { }           // missing @Test annotation\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitPublicField",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Public Fields in JUnit Test Classes",
    "description" : "Detects and disallows public fields in JUnit test classes to promote encapsulation and proper test design.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.19*\n\nChecks for public fields on a JUnit test class. There is usually no\nreason to have a public field (even a constant) on a test class.\n\nFields within interfaces and fields annotated with @Rule are ignored.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        import org.junit.Test\n        class MyTestCase {\n            public int count                        // violation\n            public static final MAX_VALUE = 1000    // violation\n\n            @Test\n            void testMe() { }\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 3,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitPublicNonTestMethod",
    "level" : "Warning",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Public Non-Test Methods in JUnit Test Classes",
    "description" : "Checks that JUnit test classes do not contain public methods other than recognized test methods or lifecycle methods annotated by JUnit.",
    "explanation" : "Rule that checks if a JUnit test class contains public methods other\nthan standard test methods, JUnit framework methods or methods with\nJUnit annotations.\n\nThe following public methods are ignored by this rule: \\* Zero-argument\nmethods with names starting with “test” \\* The `setUp()` and\n`tearDown()` methods \\* Methods annotated with `@Test` \\* Methods\nannotated with `@Before`, `@BeforeAll`, `@BeforeClass` and `@BeforeEach`\n\\* Methods annotated with `@After`, `@AfterAll`, `@AfterClass` and\n`@AfterEach` \\* Methods annotated with `@Disabled` and `@Ignore` \\*\nMethods annotated with `@Override`\n\nPublic, non-test methods on a test class violate conventional usage of\ntest classes, and they typically break encapsulation unnecessarily.\n\nPublic, non-test methods may also hide unintentional *‘Lost Tests’*. For\ninstance, the test method declaration may (unintentionally) include\nmethods parameters, and thus be ignored by JUnit. Or the method may\n(unintentionally) not follow the “test..” naming convention and not have\nthe @Test annotation, and thus be ignored by JUnit.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 40%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Default Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ignoreMethodsWithAnnotations</td>\n<td>Specifies one or more (comma-separated) annotation names. Methods\nannotated with the annotations are ignored by this rule.</td>\n<td>After,AfterAll,AfterClass, AfterEach,Before,BeforeAll,\nBeforeClass,BeforeEach, Disabled,Ignore, Override,Test</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitPublicProperty",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid Public Properties on JUnit Test Classes",
    "description" : "Detects and flags public properties defined on JUnit test classes, which are unnecessary to expose.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.21*\n\nChecks for public properties defined on JUnit test classes. There is\ntypically no need to expose a public property (with public *getter* and\n*setter* methods) on a test class.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.\n\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col style=\"width: 40%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 33%\" />\n<col style=\"width: 25%\" />\n</colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Property</th>\n<th>Description</th>\n<th>Default Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>ignorePropertyNames</td>\n<td>Specifies one or more (comma-separated) property names that should\nbe ignored (i.e., that should not cause a rule violation). The names may\noptionally contain wildcards (*,?).</td>\n<td><code>null</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\nExample of violations:\n\n        import org.junit.Test\n        class MyTestCase {\n            static String id    // violation\n            def helper          // violation\n            String name         // violation\n\n            @Test\n            void testMe() { }\n        }",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      },
      {
        "name" : "ignorePropertyNames",
        "default" : "null",
        "description" : "ignorePropertyNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitSetUpCallsSuper",
    "level" : "High",
    "category" : "ErrorProne",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "EnforceJUnitSetUpCallsSuper",
    "description" : "Ensures that if a JUnit setUp() method is defined, it calls super.setUp().",
    "explanation" : "Rule that checks that if the JUnit `setUp` method is defined, that it\nincludes a call to `super.setUp()`.\n\nThis rule ignored methods annotated with `@Before` or `@BeforeClass`.\n\nThis rule sets the default value of the *applyToClassNames* property to\nonly match class names ending in ‘Spec’, ‘Test’, ‘Tests’ or ‘TestCase’.",
    "enabled" : true,
    "timeToFix" : 5,
    "parameters" : [
      {
        "name" : "applyToClassNames",
        "default" : "*Spec,*Test,*Tests,*TestCase",
        "description" : "applyToClassNames"
      }
    ],
    "languages" : [
    ]
  },
  {
    "id" : "JUnitStyleAssertions",
    "level" : "Info",
    "category" : "BestPractice",
    "subCategory" : null,
    "title" : "Avoid JUnit Style Assertions",
    "description" : "Detects usage of JUnit style assertions such as assertEquals, assertTrue, assertFalse, assertNull, and assertNotNull in Groovy code.",
    "explanation" : "*Since CodeNarc 0.11*\n\nThis rule detects calling JUnit style assertions like `assertEquals`,\n`assertTrue`, `assertFalse`, `assertNull`, `assertNotNull`. Groovy 1.7\nships with a feature called the “power assert”, which is an assert\nstatement with better error reporting. This is preferable to the JUnit\nassertions.",
    "enabled" : true,
